#### 3. Human Analytical Narrative (The 'Why' Factor)
The upward trend in municipal waste generation from 2020 to 2025 reflects the rapid urbanization occurring across major Mindanao cities. As economic activity and population density increase, local governments face growing pressure to manage larger volumes of household and commercial waste. Although recycling and waste-diversion rates show gradual improvement, the pace of recovery remains slower than the growth of total waste produced.

This trend is linked to a significant socio-environmental reality in Mindanao: increasing urban waste contributes to clogged drainage systems, flooding during heavy rainfall, and pressure on already limited landfill facilities. The data suggest that while current waste-management programs are producing positive results, stronger investments in waste segregation, recycling infrastructure, and community-based environmental programs are necessary to support sustainable urban development in the region.
